  <section>
    <h2>Why Rockland</h2>
    <p>Rockland is the commercial hub of Midcoast Maine's Knox County — a ~7,100-resident harbor town that anchors retail, healthcare, professional services, and arts tourism for a county of roughly 40,000 people. The working waterfront (lobster, sardine-industry heritage that traces back to the nineteenth century) sits alongside one of the densest concentrations of galleries and museums north of Portland: the Farnsworth Art Museum, the Center for Maine Contemporary Art (CMCA), and the Camden Opera House a few miles north draw enough weekend arts traffic that Highbrow and Scrimshaw both report Friday-through-Sunday traffic from out-of-state visitors.</p>
    <p>The customer base is a blend the rest of Knox County cannot replicate. Census ACS data puts Rockland's median household income modestly above the state average and the population skews older than Portland — a mix of year-round working households, retiree second-home owners from Boston / New York / Connecticut, and seasonal visitors anchoring on the harbor cruise boats and the Windjammer festival. Median age in Rockland is higher than the state median, and roughly one in five Rockland housing units is classified as "seasonal" by the Census Bureau — a useful proxy for the second-home share that drives weekend store traffic.</p>
    <p>Geographically, Rockland sits on US-1 between Brunswick (45 minutes south) and Belfast (35 minutes north). That corridor carries the bulk of Midcoast tourism, and most Camden-bound traffic passes straight through Rockland. For a cannabis customer driving up the coast, Rockland is the last stop with three dispensaries on the same Main Street before Penobscot Bay narrows toward Belfast — a geography that channels Camden's spillover demand south and Belfast's spillover demand east.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Rockland's Market Context</h2>
    <p>Three dispensaries in a ~7,100-resident town is unusually dense for Maine. The ratio works out to roughly one dispensary per 2,400 residents — about double the Portland ratio (Portland has ~14 adult-use dispensaries across ~68,000 residents, or about 1 per 4,800 people). Augusta sits around 1 per 3,700, Bangor around 1 per 4,200. Rockland's density is a function of three things stacked on top of each other: Knox County's regional service-economy role, the pedestrian-friendly Main Street corridor where all three stores sit within roughly six blocks of each other, and Rockland's opt-in vote, which allowed adult-use retail well before many neighboring towns.</p>
    <p>Customer composition tracks the demographic pattern above: local year-round residents anchor the weekday trade (the single largest share), weekend second-home owners and arts-tourism visitors drive Friday-through-Sunday traffic, and Camden's spillover demand (Camden has zero dispensaries within town limits, see <a href="/guides/camden-dispensary-guide">Camden dispensary guide</a>) flows south roughly five-to-seven miles to Main Street. Coastal visitors mixing harbor walks with dispensary stops are a smaller but consistent share — Rockland does not get the volume of pure tourist traffic that Bar Harbor does, but it gets a steadier mix of customers who came for the town itself and treat dispensary visits as part of the trip.</p>
    <p>What makes Rockland's market distinct is the walkable retail cluster itself. This is the only Maine town where a customer can comparison-shop three dispensaries on foot inside a single afternoon. Each operator differentiates: Highbrow for the widest selection and statewide brand consistency, Scrimshaw for focused medical patient service, Botany for an effect-based product classification system (Up / Relaxed / Focused / Social / Balanced) that appeals to first-time buyers who do not want to think in strain names. The competition is real but it does not collapse prices — Maine's adult-use tax structure and licensing caps keep pricing statewide, so the differentiator is service and selection rather than price.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Visiting Rockland Dispensaries</h2>
    <p>All three Rockland dispensaries cluster on Main Street between roughly 400 and 700 Main — walkable from any downtown parking. Metered street parking is available on Main; public lots sit within two blocks (the most-used are behind the Farnsworth and the parking area near the harbor). Because the cluster is tight, plan to park once and walk, especially on summer Saturdays when harbor-event traffic tightens curbside spots.</p>
    <p><strong>Payment.</strong> Maine dispensary norm is cash or debit-card processed through Hypur or Vermont Patient Products Compliance (VTPC). All three Rockland stores run on-site ATMs; expect a small ATM fee (commonly $2–$3). Credit cards are not accepted at any Maine adult-use dispensary by state regulation.</p>
    <p><strong>What to bring.</strong> A valid state photo ID (Maine or any U.S. state) for adult-use purchases. Medical patients should bring a Maine medical card (or a valid card from a reciprocity state — Maine currently accepts cards from all neighboring states; check the OCP reciprocity list before driving in if you are out of state). Cash is faster than debit.</p>
    <p><strong>Hours pattern.</strong> Scrimshaw Cannabis closes 6 pm daily — the earliest of the three. Botany closes 7 pm most nights. Highbrow runs the latest hours, sometimes to 8 pm on weeknights. None of the three close for the winter — Rockland's year-round demand keeps the stores open across all four seasons.</p>
    <p><strong>Accessibility.</strong> All three storefronts are single-floor Main Street retail and ADA-accessible. Highbrow and Botany both offer curbside pickup for online orders; Scrimshaw serves medical patients inside the store. None of the three currently run home delivery — Rockland-area customers seeking delivery should treat it as a Portland-or-Augusta option, not a Midcoast one.</p>
  </section>

